HELP_TEXT = '''
WHAT THIS SCRIPT DOES
  Records from your default audio source and applies edits to WAV or FLAC
  files. Each edit is written to a new numbered file, played in mpv, and
  you decide what happens next. Your starting file or recording is never
  changed or deleted.

HOW TO RUN
  audio_edit.py                  record a new take, then edit it
  audio_edit.py --meter          live level meter first, then record
  audio_edit.py .                pick a file from the current folder
  audio_edit.py FOLDER           pick a file from FOLDER
  audio_edit.py FILE.wav         work on that file directly
  audio_edit.py --organize       move old recordings into day folders
  audio_edit.py --album FOLDER   master a whole album with ONE shared gain
  audio_edit.py --keep-all ...   keep every render of the session
  audio_edit.py --plain ...      plain mpv window, no meter or spectrum

RECORDING
  Run with no file or folder. Press any key to start, any key to stop.
  Esc at the first prompt cancels.
  Recordings are saved as FLAC in a folder per day:
      $HOME/Documents/audio_edit_recordings/YYYYMMDD/
  Edits are saved next to the file they came from. To move older files
  into day folders, run once:
      audio_edit.py --organize
  The date is read from the file name (recording_YYYYMMDD_...). Existing
  files are never overwritten.
  To pick a file from one day:
      audio_edit.py ~/Documents/audio_edit_recordings/20261111

  The script records from the CURRENT DEFAULT SOURCE. Check it before
  running with:
      pactl get-default-source
  Change it with pactl set-default-source NAME, or in your sound settings.
  Recording uses parec (package libpulse) for 24-bit capture, encoded to
  FLAC by ffmpeg. Without parec it falls back to ffmpeg alone, 16-bit,
  with a timer but no level meter.

  LIVE METER (with parec)
  While recording you see a bar and a dB value for the left and right
  channels, plus a peak hold that stays for 1.5 seconds. CLIP appears
  when a peak reaches -0.5 dB. Aim for peaks around -12 to -6 dB.
  After stopping you get the highest peak and the number of clipped
  moments.

  METER MODE (--meter)
  Shows the same live meter without saving anything, so you can set the
  levels on your mixer and interface. Press any key to stop it.

  AFTER A RECORDING
  mpv plays the take, then you choose:
      k  keep and edit    d  discard (deletes the file)
      r  re-record        l  listen again

MASTERING (finished projects)
  Goal: no digital clipping on other people's devices and formats, with
  the sound unchanged.
  1. Render from your DAW at 32-bit float, no limiter on the master.
  2. peak-report shows peak, crest factor and what cutting would do.
  3. normalize (-1 dB) is the safe default for a single track.
  4. ceiling-clip cuts only the peaks above a level, then sets the final
     peak. Use it when a few spikes set the level. To only cut, without
     changing the level, set clip level 0 and final peak 0.
  5. For an ALBUM do not normalize tracks one by one: every track would
     end up with the same peak, and quiet tracks would become loud.
     Use:  audio_edit.py --album FOLDER
     It finds the highest peak of all tracks, applies the same dB change
     to every track, and writes 24-bit copies into FOLDER/mastered/.
     Relative levels between tracks stay as you composed them. The
     originals are never changed. --album-peak DB sets the final peak of
     the loudest track (default -1).

OVERDUB (record on top of the current version)
  Choose "overdub" in the edit menu. The current version plays while the
  default source is recorded. Press any key to start, any key to stop.
  The new take is mixed onto the current version and becomes a normal
  edit: keep it, then overdub again to stack more layers. undo removes
  the last layer.
  Settings:
      level of the new recording (dB)
      level of the existing sound (dB)
      latency compensation (ms): trims the start of the new take so it
          lines up. Measure it by overdubbing a clap in time with the
          playback and checking how late it lands. Remembered for the
          session.
      loop the existing sound: repeat it while recording, like a looper.
          The result is as long as the take.
  Use headphones, or the speakers will be recorded too. The take is
  added to the existing sound, so check the level afterwards.

CHANNEL SWITCH (stereo files only)
  Plays the sound from one channel only, then the other, then returns to
  stereo. The pattern can repeat until the end of the file.
  Settings:
      start with which channel (l or r)
      start at: before this time the sound stays in stereo
      first channel only (seconds)
      second channel only (seconds), defaults to the previous time
      back to stereo (seconds), defaults to the previous time
      cycles: 0 repeats until the end, 1 plays the pattern once
      transition (ms): crossfade at each switch, no clicks
  A channel playing alone carries the mono mix of both sides.

FILE PICKER
  Not recursive: lists only WAV and FLAC files directly inside the folder,
  newest first. The preview pane shows duration, sample rate, channels,
  format and size.
  Moving the cursor over a file PLAYS it. Moving to another file stops the
  first and plays the new one. Leaving the picker stops playback.
  Enter selects. Ctrl-R plays the highlighted file again from the start.
  Esc cancels.

VERSIONS AND WHAT IS SAVED
  The session keeps a history of kept versions:
      starting file -> edit 1 -> edit 2 -> edit 3
  The version you are working on is always shown at the top of the edit
  menu and before each edit.

  After each render:
      k  keep       add it to the history and keep editing
      f  finish     keep it and end the session
      d  discard    delete it, go back to the version shown in the prompt
      r  run again  delete it, repeat the same edit with new answers
      a  another    delete it, choose a different edit
      l  listen     play the render again
      q  quit       delete it and end the session with the previous version

  In the edit menu (bottom of the list):
      undo    delete the last kept edit and go back one version
              (repeat to go back further, down to the starting file)
      finish  end the session
  Esc in the edit menu also ends the session.

  When the session ends, every intermediate version is deleted. Only the
  last version and your starting file remain. The final line shows its
  path: "Final file: ...". Use --keep-all to keep every render.

TIME FORMAT
  Shown like mpv: HH:MM:SS, with .mmm only when needed.
  You can type: 4   4.5   17:54   00:17:54   00:17:54.320

EDITS
  extract        keep a time range
  trim-silence   remove silence at the start and end
  reverse        play the file backwards
  fade           cosine fade in and fade out, in seconds
  normalize      set the peak level in dB
  gain-up        +6 dB, clipped at full scale
  gain-down      -6 dB
  gain-crush     drive into hard clipping, then trim to 0.85 (default 18 dB)
  denoise        reduce steady hiss using a noise sample
  declick        remove short clicks and crackle spikes
  declip         rebuild flat-topped (clipped) peaks, lowers level slightly
  remove-dc      remove DC offset (a steady shift of the waveform away from
                 zero). It does not fix hiss or crackle.
  varispeed      change speed and pitch together, like tape
  stretch        Paulstretch-style stretch, up to 30 minutes of output
  stereo-width   mid/side width, 0 = mono, 1 = unchanged
  loop           repeat the whole file N times, optional crossfade
  loop-section   loop a range N times, then continue with the file
                 example: 00:11 to 00:14, 4 times
  layer          mix another file from the same folder on top
  overdub        record live on top of the current version
  channel-switch one channel only, then the other, then stereo, for times
                 you set. You choose which channel starts. Can repeat.
  peak-report    facts about peaks and loudness. Makes no file.
  ceiling-clip   cut only the peaks above a level, then set the final peak

MPV WINDOW
  After each render mpv shows level bars with dB values above a scrolling
  spectrum. Hiss shows as a steady bright band near the top. Crackle
  shows as vertical streaks. Press i in mpv for file information.
  Use --plain if the window stays blank.

SUGGESTED ORDER FOR A NOISY RECORDING
  extract, declip, declick (start at 15), denoise (6 to 10 dB, noise
  sample from a hiss-only gap), normalize.
  Crackle that changes with volume is often clipping somewhere in the
  chain. Lower levels at the source if you can.
  Run declip before gain-crush, never after.
'''


class EditError(Exception):
    """Raised by an edit when it cannot run; the script carries on."""


def fail(message: str) -> None:
    print(f"audio_edit: {message}", file=sys.stderr)
    raise SystemExit(1)


# ---------- time helpers ----------

def parse_time(text: str) -> float:
    """Accepts 4, 4.5, 17:54, 00:17:54 and 00:17:54.320"""
    text = text.strip()
    if not text:
        raise ValueError("empty")
    seconds = 0.0
    for part in text.split(":"):
        seconds = seconds * 60.0 + float(part)
    return seconds


def format_time(seconds: float) -> str:
    """HH:MM:SS, with .mmm added only when milliseconds are not zero."""
    total_ms = max(0, int(round(seconds * 1000)))
    h, rem = divmod(total_ms, 3_600_000)
    m, rem = divmod(rem, 60_000)
    s, ms = divmod(rem, 1000)
    text = f"{h:02d}:{m:02d}:{s:02d}"
    if ms:
        text += f".{ms:03d}"
    return text


# ---------- prompts ----------

def ask(prompt: str, default: float, minimum: float, maximum: float | None,
        is_time: bool = False) -> float:
    shown = format_time(default) if is_time else f"{default:g}"
    while True:
        try:
            answer = input(f"{prompt} [{shown}]: ").strip()
        except EOFError:
            raise SystemExit(0)
        try:
            if not answer:
                value = default
            elif is_time:
                value = parse_time(answer)
            else:
                value = float(answer)
        except ValueError:
            print("Enter seconds or HH:MM:SS.mmm." if is_time else "Enter a number.")
            continue
        if value < minimum:
            print(f"Must be at least {minimum:g}.")
            continue
        if maximum is not None and value > maximum:
            print(f"Must be at most {maximum:g}.")
            continue
        return value


# ---------- audio helpers ----------

def load_audio(path: Path) -> tuple[np.ndarray, int]:
    try:
        audio, rate = sf.read(path, always_2d=True, dtype="float32")
    except Exception as error:
        fail(f"Could not read {path.name}: {error}")
    if len(audio) == 0:
        fail(f"No audio frames in: {path.name}")
    return audio, rate


def save_audio(path: Path, audio: np.ndarray, rate: int) -> None:
    fmt = "FLAC" if path.suffix.lower() == ".flac" else "WAV"
    try:
        sf.write(path, audio, rate, format=fmt, subtype="PCM_24")
    except Exception as error:
        fail(f"Could not write {path.name}: {error}")


def next_output(path: Path, process: str) -> Path:
    suffix = ".flac" if path.suffix.lower() == ".flac" else ".wav"
    name = process.replace("-", "_")
    n = 1
    while True:
        candidate = path.with_name(f"{path.stem}_{name}_{n:02d}{suffix}")
        if not candidate.exists():
            return candidate
        n += 1


def match_channels(audio: np.ndarray, channels: int) -> np.ndarray:
    if audio.shape[1] == channels:
        return audio
    if audio.shape[1] > channels:
        return audio[:, :channels].copy()
    return np.repeat(audio[:, :1], channels, axis=1)


def resample(audio: np.ndarray, speed: float) -> np.ndarray:
    """Linear-interpolation varispeed. speed 0.5 = half speed, longer, lower."""
    n_out = max(1, int(len(audio) / speed))
    positions = np.linspace(0, len(audio) - 1, n_out)
    index = np.arange(len(audio))
    return np.stack(
        [np.interp(positions, index, audio[:, c]) for c in range(audio.shape[1])],
        axis=1,
    ).astype(np.float32)


def crossfade_join(pieces: list[np.ndarray], xf: int) -> np.ndarray:
    """Join pieces end to end, overlapping each join by xf samples."""
    pieces = [p for p in pieces if len(p)]
    if not pieces:
        raise EditError("Nothing to join.")
    if len(pieces) == 1:
        return pieces[0].copy()
    xf = min(xf, min(len(p) for p in pieces) // 2)
    if xf <= 0:
        return np.concatenate(pieces).astype(np.float32)

    t = np.linspace(0.0, np.pi / 2, xf, dtype=np.float32)
    fade_in = np.sin(t)[:, None]
    fade_out = np.cos(t)[:, None]

    total = sum(len(p) for p in pieces) - xf * (len(pieces) - 1)
    out = np.zeros((total, pieces[0].shape[1]), dtype=np.float32)
    pos = 0
    for i, piece in enumerate(pieces):
        piece = piece.copy()
        if i > 0:
            piece[:xf] *= fade_in
        if i < len(pieces) - 1:
            piece[-xf:] *= fade_out
        out[pos:pos + len(piece)] += piece
        pos += len(piece) - xf
    return out


# ---------- hover preview (used by fzf) ----------

def stop_preview() -> None:
    """Stop the background preview player, if one is running."""
    try:
        pid = int(PREVIEW_PID.read_text().strip())
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return
    try:
        cmdline = Path(f"/proc/{pid}/cmdline").read_bytes()
        if b"mpv" in cmdline:
            os.kill(pid, signal.SIGTERM)
    except OSError:
        pass
    PREVIEW_PID.unlink(missing_ok=True)


def start_preview(path_text: str) -> None:
    """Stop any running preview and play this file in the background."""
    stop_preview()
    path = Path(path_text).expanduser().resolve()
    if not path.is_file():
        return
    try:
        proc = subprocess.Popen(
            ["mpv", "--no-video", "--no-terminal", "--really-quiet",
             "--audio-display=no", "--", str(path)],
            stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL,
            stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, start_new_session=True)
        PREVIEW_PID.write_text(str(proc.pid))
    except OSError:
        pass


# ---------- recording ----------

def default_source() -> str | None:
    try:
        result = subprocess.run(["pactl", "get-default-source"],
                                capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=3)
    except (FileNotFoundError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return None
    return result.stdout.strip() or None


def read_key() -> bytes:
    """Wait for one key press without needing Enter. Returns the first byte."""
    fd = sys.stdin.fileno()
    old = termios.tcgetattr(fd)
    try:
        tty.setcbreak(fd)
        key = os.read(fd, 1)
        extra = False
        while select.select([fd], [], [], 0.03)[0]:
            os.read(fd, 16)
            extra = True
    finally:
        termios.tcsetattr(fd, termios.TCSADRAIN, old)
    if key == b"\x1b" and extra:
        return b"?"          # arrow keys and similar are not Esc
    return key


def to_db(value: float) -> float:
    return 20.0 * math.log10(max(value, 1e-6))


def meter_bar(db: float, width: int = 14) -> str:
    """Bar from -60 dB (empty) to 0 dB (full)."""
    filled = int(round(max(0.0, min(1.0, (db + 60.0) / 60.0)) * width))
    return "#" * filled + "-" * (width - filled)


def capture_loop(parec, ffmpeg, state: dict, chunks: list | None = None) -> None:
    """Read audio from parec, measure peaks, and pass it on.

    Data goes to ffmpeg (if given) and/or into the chunks list (if given)."""
    chunk = RECORD_RATE * 2 * 4 // 20          # 50 ms of 32-bit stereo
    try:
        while True:
            data = parec.stdout.read(chunk)
            if not data:
                break
            usable = len(data) - (len(data) % 8)
            if usable:
                samples = np.frombuffer(data[:usable], dtype="<i4").reshape(-1, 2)
                peaks = np.abs(samples.astype(np.int64)).max(axis=0) / 2147483648.0
                state["win"] = np.maximum(state["win"], peaks)
                state["max"] = max(state["max"], float(peaks.max()))
                if float(peaks.max()) >= 0.944:        # -0.5 dB
                    state["clips"] += 1
                if chunks is not None:
                    chunks.append(data[:usable])
            if ffmpeg is not None:
                try:
                    ffmpeg.stdin.write(data)
                except (BrokenPipeError, ValueError, OSError):
                    break
    finally:
        if ffmpeg is not None:
            try:
                ffmpeg.stdin.close()
            except (BrokenPipeError, ValueError, OSError):
                pass


def finish_recording(parec, ffmpeg, thread) -> str:
    """Stop capture and let ffmpeg finish the FLAC cleanly."""
    if parec is not None:
        if parec.poll() is None:
            parec.terminate()
        try:
            parec.wait(timeout=3)
        except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
            parec.kill()
        if thread is not None:
            thread.join(timeout=5)
    elif ffmpeg.poll() is None:
        ffmpeg.send_signal(2)        # SIGINT: ffmpeg finalizes the file
    try:
        ffmpeg.wait(timeout=20)
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        ffmpeg.kill()
        ffmpeg.wait()
    try:
        return (ffmpeg.stderr.read() or b"").decode(errors="replace").strip()
    except (ValueError, OSError):
        return ""


def meter_mode() -> None:
    """Show the live level meter without recording. Any key stops it."""
    if not sys.stdin.isatty():
        fail("Meter mode needs an interactive terminal.")
    if not shutil.which("parec"):
        fail("Meter mode needs parec (package libpulse).")

    print("\nMETER (nothing is recorded)")
    print(f"Source: {default_source() or 'default'}")
    print("Aim for peaks around -12 to -6 dB. Press any key to stop.\n")

    state = {"win": np.zeros(2), "max": 0.0, "clips": 0}
    parec = subprocess.Popen(
        ["parec", "--format=s32le", f"--rate={RECORD_RATE}", "--channels=2"],
        stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL)
    thread = threading.Thread(target=capture_loop, args=(parec, None, state),
                              daemon=True)
    thread.start()

    fd = sys.stdin.fileno()
    old = termios.tcgetattr(fd)
    hold = [-90.0, -90.0]
    hold_time = [0.0, 0.0]
    try:
        tty.setcbreak(fd)
        while True:
            now = time.time()
            current = state["win"].copy()
            state["win"] = np.zeros(2)
            parts = []
            for ch in range(2):
                db = to_db(float(current[ch]))
                if db >= hold[ch] or now - hold_time[ch] > 1.5:
                    hold[ch] = db
                    hold_time[ch] = now
                label = "L" if ch == 0 else "R"
                parts.append(f"{label} {meter_bar(db)} {db:6.1f}")
            warn = "  CLIP" if max(hold) >= -0.5 else ""
            print(f"\r  {parts[0]}  {parts[1]}  hold {hold[0]:5.1f} {hold[1]:5.1f}"
                  f"  max {to_db(state['max']):5.1f}{warn}\x1b[K", end="", flush=True)
            if select.select([fd], [], [], 0.1)[0]:
                os.read(fd, 16)
                break
            if parec.poll() is not None:
                break
    except KeyboardInterrupt:
        pass
    finally:
        termios.tcsetattr(fd, termios.TCSADRAIN, old)
        if parec.poll() is None:
            parec.terminate()
        try:
            parec.wait(timeout=3)
        except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
            parec.kill()
        thread.join(timeout=3)
    print(f"\n\nHighest peak: {to_db(state['max']):.1f} dB")


def record_take() -> Path | None:
    if not sys.stdin.isatty():
        fail("Recording needs an interactive terminal.")
    if not shutil.which("ffmpeg"):
        fail("ffmpeg was not found in PATH.")

    use_parec = shutil.which("parec") is not None
    source = default_source()

    print("\nRECORD")
    print(f"Source:   {source or 'default (could not read name with pactl)'}")
    print(f"Quality:  {'24-bit' if use_parec else '16-bit (install libpulse for 24-bit)'}"
          f" FLAC, {RECORD_RATE} Hz, stereo")
    print(f"Meter:    {'on' if use_parec else 'off (needs parec)'}")
    print(f"Saves to: {RECORD_DIR / datetime.date.today().strftime('%Y%m%d')}")
    print("\nPress any key to start recording. Esc cancels.")
    if read_key() == b"\x1b":
        return None

    stamp = datetime.datetime.now().strftime("%Y%m%d_%H%M%S")
    day_dir = RECORD_DIR / stamp[:8]
    day_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    path = day_dir / f"recording_{stamp}.flac"

    state = {"win": np.zeros(2), "max": 0.0, "clips": 0}
    parec = None
    thread = None
    base = ["ffmpeg", "-hide_banner", "-loglevel", "error", "-nostdin", "-y"]

    if use_parec:
        parec = subprocess.Popen(
            ["parec", "--format=s32le", f"--rate={RECORD_RATE}", "--channels=2"],
            stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL)
        ffmpeg = subprocess.Popen(
            base + ["-f", "s32le", "-ar", str(RECORD_RATE), "-ac", "2",
                    "-i", "pipe:0", "-c:a", "flac", "-sample_fmt", "s32",
                    "-bits_per_raw_sample", "24", str(path)],
            stdin=subprocess.PIPE, stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL,
            stderr=subprocess.PIPE)
        thread = threading.Thread(target=capture_loop,
                                  args=(parec, ffmpeg, state), daemon=True)
        thread.start()
    else:
        ffmpeg = subprocess.Popen(
            base + ["-f", "pulse", "-i", "default", "-c:a", "flac", str(path)],
            stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL,
            stderr=subprocess.PIPE)

    time.sleep(0.5)
    if ffmpeg.poll() is not None or (parec is not None and parec.poll() is not None):
        message = finish_recording(parec, ffmpeg, thread)
        path.unlink(missing_ok=True)
        fail("Recording could not start. Check the default source with "
             f"'pactl get-default-source'. {message}")

    fd = sys.stdin.fileno()
    old = termios.tcgetattr(fd)
    started = time.time()
    hold = [-90.0, -90.0]
    hold_time = [0.0, 0.0]
    interrupted = False

    try:
        tty.setcbreak(fd)
        print("Recording... press any key to stop.\n")
        while True:
            now = time.time()
            line = f"\r  {format_time(now - started)}  "
            if use_parec:
                current = state["win"].copy()
                state["win"] = np.zeros(2)
                parts = []
                for ch in range(2):
                    db = to_db(float(current[ch]))
                    if db >= hold[ch] or now - hold_time[ch] > 1.5:
                        hold[ch] = db
                        hold_time[ch] = now
                    label = "L" if ch == 0 else "R"
                    parts.append(f"{label} {meter_bar(db)} {db:6.1f}")
                warn = "  CLIP" if max(hold) >= -0.5 else ""
                line += (f"{parts[0]}  {parts[1]}  "
                         f"hold {hold[0]:5.1f} {hold[1]:5.1f}{warn}")
            print(line + "\x1b[K", end="", flush=True)

            if select.select([fd], [], [], 0.1)[0]:
                os.read(fd, 16)
                break
            if ffmpeg.poll() is not None:
                break
    except KeyboardInterrupt:
        interrupted = True
    finally:
        termios.tcsetattr(fd, termios.TCSADRAIN, old)
        message = finish_recording(parec, ffmpeg, thread)

    print(f"\n\nStopped. Length: {format_time(time.time() - started)}")
    if use_parec:
        print(f"Highest peak: {to_db(state['max']):.1f} dB")
        if state["clips"]:
            print(f"Clipped moments: {state['clips']} (peaks at or above -0.5 dB). "
                  "Lower the level at the source and record again.")
        else:
            print("No clipping detected.")

    if not path.exists() or path.stat().st_size < 1000:
        path.unlink(missing_ok=True)
        fail(f"Nothing was recorded. {message}")

    print(f"Saved: {path}")
    if interrupted:
        print("(Stopped with Ctrl-C; the take was kept.)")
    return path


# ---------- basic edits ----------

def do_extract(audio, rate):
    duration = len(audio) / rate
    print(f"\nDuration: {format_time(duration)}")
    start = ask("Start", 0.0, 0.0, max(0.0, duration - 0.001), is_time=True)
    end = ask("End", duration, start + 0.001, duration, is_time=True)
    return audio[int(round(start * rate)):int(round(end * rate))].copy()


def do_trim_silence(audio, rate):
    threshold_db = ask("Silence threshold (dB)", -50, -120, -1)
    pad_ms = ask("Keep padding (ms)", 20, 0, 5000)
    threshold = 10.0 ** (threshold_db / 20.0)
    loud = np.flatnonzero(np.max(np.abs(audio), axis=1) >= threshold)
    if len(loud) == 0:
        raise EditError("Everything is below the threshold. Try a lower value.")
    pad = int(pad_ms * rate / 1000)
    start = max(0, loud[0] - pad)
    end = min(len(audio), loud[-1] + 1 + pad)
    print(f"Removed {format_time(start / rate)} from the start and "
          f"{format_time((len(audio) - end) / rate)} from the end.")
    return audio[start:end].copy()


def do_reverse(audio, rate):
    return audio[::-1].copy()


def do_fade(audio, rate):
    duration = len(audio) / rate
    print(f"\nDuration: {format_time(duration)}")
    print("Times are in seconds. Enter 0 to skip a fade.")
    fade_in = ask("Fade in (seconds)", min(2.0, duration), 0.0, duration)
    remaining = duration - fade_in
    fade_out = ask("Fade out (seconds)", min(2.0, remaining), 0.0, remaining)

    envelope = np.ones(len(audio), dtype=np.float32)
    n_in = int(round(fade_in * rate))
    if n_in > 1:
        ramp = 0.5 - 0.5 * np.cos(np.linspace(0.0, np.pi, n_in))
        envelope[:n_in] = ramp.astype(np.float32)
    n_out = int(round(fade_out * rate))
    if n_out > 1:
        ramp = 0.5 - 0.5 * np.cos(np.linspace(0.0, np.pi, n_out))
        envelope[-n_out:] *= ramp[::-1].astype(np.float32)
    return audio * envelope[:, None]


def do_normalize(audio, rate):
    peak = float(np.max(np.abs(audio)))
    if peak <= 0:
        raise EditError("The file is silent.")
    print(f"Current peak: {20 * math.log10(peak):.2f} dB")
    target_db = ask("Target peak (dB)", -1.0, -60, 0)
    return audio * ((10.0 ** (target_db / 20.0)) / peak)


def do_gain_up(audio, rate):
    return audio * (10.0 ** (6.0 / 20.0))


def do_gain_down(audio, rate):
    return audio * (10.0 ** (-6.0 / 20.0))


def do_gain_crush(audio, rate):
    print("""
GAIN CRUSH
Boosts the signal far past full scale and hard clips it. The waveform
tops are cut flat, which adds harsh distortion. The result is then
lowered to 0.85 so it does not hit full scale.

  Drive (dB): how much the signal is boosted before clipping.
    6-12   = mild, crunchy edge on loud parts only.
    18     = heavy distortion. Your original setting.
    24-36  = nearly a square wave, dense and harsh.
    40+    = extreme. Everything becomes a wall of clipped noise.
""")
    drive_db = ask("Drive in dB", 18, 0, 60)
    driven = audio * (10.0 ** (drive_db / 20.0))
    clipped = np.clip(driven, -1.0, 1.0)
    return clipped * 0.85


def do_remove_dc(audio, rate):
    offsets = np.mean(audio, axis=0)
    print("DC offset per channel: " + ", ".join(f"{v:+.5f}" for v in offsets))
    n = len(audio)
    spectrum = np.fft.rfft(audio, axis=0)
    freqs = np.fft.rfftfreq(n, 1.0 / rate)
    t = np.clip((freqs - 1.0) / 3.0, 0.0, 1.0)      # 0 below 1 Hz, 1 above 4 Hz
    gain = (0.5 - 0.5 * np.cos(np.pi * t))[:, None]
    return np.fft.irfft(spectrum * gain, n=n, axis=0).astype(np.float32)


def do_varispeed(audio, rate):
    speed = ask("Speed (0.5 = half speed, 2 = double)", 0.5, 0.05, 8)
    print(f"Pitch change: {12 * math.log2(speed):+.2f} semitones")
    return resample(audio, speed)


def paulstretch_channel(x: np.ndarray, n: int, stretch: float) -> np.ndarray:
    half = n // 2
    window = (1.0 - np.linspace(-1.0, 1.0, n) ** 2) ** 1.25
    step = half / stretch
    frames = int(len(x) / step) + 1
    padded = np.concatenate([x, np.zeros(n, dtype=x.dtype)])
    out = np.zeros(frames * half + n, dtype=np.float32)
    for i in range(frames):
        pos = int(i * step)
        frame = padded[pos:pos + n] * window
        magnitude = np.abs(np.fft.rfft(frame))
        phase = np.random.uniform(0.0, 2.0 * np.pi, len(magnitude))
        new = np.fft.irfft(magnitude * np.exp(1j * phase), n) * window
        out[i * half:i * half + n] += new
    return out[:int(len(x) * stretch)]


def do_stretch(audio, rate):
    duration = len(audio) / rate
    max_stretch = max(1.0, 1800.0 / duration)        # keep output under 30 minutes
    print(f"\nDuration: {format_time(duration)}")
    stretch = ask("Stretch factor (8 = eight times longer)",
                  min(8.0, max_stretch), 1.0, max_stretch)
    window_s = ask("Window size in seconds (larger = smoother)", 0.35, 0.02, 5.0)
    print(f"Result length: {format_time(duration * stretch)}")

    n = max(64, int(window_s * rate))
    n -= n % 2
    channels = [paulstretch_channel(audio[:, c], n, stretch)
                for c in range(audio.shape[1])]
    out = np.stack(channels, axis=1)

    peak_in = float(np.max(np.abs(audio)))
    peak_out = float(np.max(np.abs(out)))
    if peak_out > 0:
        out *= peak_in / peak_out
    return out


def do_stereo_width(audio, rate):
    if audio.shape[1] < 2:
        raise EditError("Stereo width needs a stereo file.")
    width = ask("Width (0 = mono, 1 = unchanged, 2 = twice as wide)", 1.5, 0, 4)
    left, right = audio[:, 0], audio[:, 1]
    mid = (left + right) / 2.0
    side = (left - right) / 2.0 * width
    out = audio.copy()
    out[:, 0] = mid + side
    out[:, 1] = mid - side
    return out


# ---------- looping, layering and overdubbing ----------

def do_loop(audio, rate):
    duration = len(audio) / rate
    print(f"\nDuration: {format_time(duration)}")
    times = int(ask("Number of times to play the file", 2, 1, 1000))
    xf_ms = ask("Crossfade between repeats (ms, 0 = none)", 0, 0, 5000)
    xf = int(xf_ms * rate / 1000)
    result = crossfade_join([audio] * times, xf)
    print(f"Result length: {format_time(len(result) / rate)}")
    return result


def do_loop_section(audio, rate):
    duration = len(audio) / rate
    print(f"\nDuration: {format_time(duration)}")
    start = ask("Section start", 0.0, 0.0, max(0.0, duration - 0.001), is_time=True)
    end = ask("Section end", duration, start + 0.001, duration, is_time=True)
    times = int(ask("Times the section plays in total", 4, 1, 1000))
    xf_ms = ask("Crossfade at the joins (ms)", 5, 0, 500)

    s = int(round(start * rate))
    e = int(round(end * rate))
    pieces = [audio[:s]] + [audio[s:e]] * times + [audio[e:]]
    result = crossfade_join(pieces, int(xf_ms * rate / 1000))
    print(f"Result length: {format_time(len(result) / rate)}")
    return result


def do_layer(audio, rate):
    print("\nChoose the file to layer on top.")
    other_path = choose_file(CONTEXT["folder"], "Layer> ")
    if other_path is None:
        raise EditError("No layer file chosen.")
    other, other_rate = load_audio(other_path)
    if other_rate != rate:
        other = resample(other, other_rate / rate)
    other = match_channels(other, audio.shape[1])

    gain_db = ask("Layer level (dB)", -6, -60, 12)
    offset = ask("Layer starts at (seconds)", 0, 0, len(audio) / rate)
    try:
        loop_it = input("Loop the layer to fill the file? [y/N]: ").strip().lower() == "y"
    except EOFError:
        raise SystemExit(0)

    start = int(offset * rate)
    other = other * (10.0 ** (gain_db / 20.0))
    if loop_it:
        remaining = len(audio) - start
        reps = math.ceil(remaining / len(other))
        other = np.tile(other, (reps, 1))[:remaining]

    total = max(len(audio), start + len(other))
    out = np.zeros((total, audio.shape[1]), dtype=np.float32)
    out[:len(audio)] += audio
    out[start:start + len(other)] += other
    return out


def do_overdub(audio, rate):
    if not sys.stdin.isatty():
        raise EditError("Overdub needs an interactive terminal.")
    if not shutil.which("parec"):
        raise EditError("Overdub needs parec (package libpulse).")
    if not shutil.which("mpv"):
        raise EditError("mpv was not found in PATH.")

    duration = len(audio) / rate
    print(f"""
OVERDUB
The current version plays while your default source is recorded. The new
take is mixed on top. Use headphones so the playback is not recorded.

Current version length: {format_time(duration)}
Source: {default_source() or 'default'}

  Level of the new recording (dB): 0 keeps it as recorded.
  Level of the existing sound (dB): lower it to make room for the layer.
  Latency compensation (ms): trims the start of the new take so it lines
    up. If your layers land late, raise it. To measure: overdub a clap in
    time with the playback and see how late it lands. Start with 0.
  Loop the existing sound: repeats it while you record, like a looper.
    The result is as long as your take. Answer n to play it once.
""")
    new_db = ask("Level of the new recording (dB)", 0, -60, 12)
    base_db = ask("Level of the existing sound (dB)", 0, -60, 12)
    latency_ms = ask("Latency compensation (ms)", CONTEXT["latency_ms"], 0, 2000)
    CONTEXT["latency_ms"] = latency_ms
    try:
        loop_it = input("Loop the existing sound while recording? [y/N]: "
                        ).strip().lower() == "y"
    except EOFError:
        raise SystemExit(0)

    print("\nPress any key to start playback and recording. Esc cancels.")
    if read_key() == b"\x1b":
        raise EditError("Cancelled.")

    state = {"win": np.zeros(2), "max": 0.0, "clips": 0}
    chunks: list[bytes] = []

    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:
        base_path = Path(tmp) / "base.wav"
        sf.write(base_path, audio, rate, format="WAV", subtype="FLOAT")

        parec = subprocess.Popen(
            ["parec", "--format=s32le", f"--rate={RECORD_RATE}", "--channels=2"],
            stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL)
        thread = threading.Thread(target=capture_loop,
                                  args=(parec, None, state, chunks), daemon=True)
        thread.start()

        player_cmd = ["mpv", "--no-video", "--no-terminal", "--really-quiet",
                      "--audio-display=no"]
        if loop_it:
            player_cmd.append("--loop-file=inf")
        player = subprocess.Popen(
            player_cmd + ["--", str(base_path)],
            stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL,
            stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL)

        time.sleep(0.4)
        if parec.poll() is not None:
            player.terminate()
            raise EditError("Recording could not start. Check the default source "
                            "with 'pactl get-default-source'.")

        fd = sys.stdin.fileno()
        old = termios.tcgetattr(fd)
        started = time.time()
        hold = [-90.0, -90.0]
        hold_time = [0.0, 0.0]
        try:
            tty.setcbreak(fd)
            print("Recording... press any key to stop.\n")
            while True:
                now = time.time()
                current = state["win"].copy()
                state["win"] = np.zeros(2)
                parts = []
                for ch in range(2):
                    db = to_db(float(current[ch]))
                    if db >= hold[ch] or now - hold_time[ch] > 1.5:
                        hold[ch] = db
                        hold_time[ch] = now
                    label = "L" if ch == 0 else "R"
                    parts.append(f"{label} {meter_bar(db)} {db:6.1f}")
                warn = "  CLIP" if max(hold) >= -0.5 else ""
                print(f"\r  {format_time(now - started)}  {parts[0]}  {parts[1]}"
                      f"  hold {hold[0]:5.1f} {hold[1]:5.1f}{warn}\x1b[K",
                      end="", flush=True)
                if select.select([fd], [], [], 0.1)[0]:
                    os.read(fd, 16)
                    break
                if parec.poll() is not None:
                    break
        except KeyboardInterrupt:
            pass
        finally:
            termios.tcsetattr(fd, termios.TCSADRAIN, old)
            if player.poll() is None:
                player.terminate()
            try:
                player.wait(timeout=3)
            except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
                player.kill()
            if parec.poll() is None:
                parec.terminate()
            try:
                parec.wait(timeout=3)
            except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
                parec.kill()
            thread.join(timeout=5)

    print(f"\n\nStopped. Length: {format_time(time.time() - started)}")
    print(f"Highest peak of the take: {to_db(state['max']):.1f} dB")

    data = b"".join(chunks)
    data = data[:len(data) - (len(data) % 8)]
    if not data:
        raise EditError("Nothing was recorded.")
    new = np.frombuffer(data, dtype="<i4").reshape(-1, 2).astype(np.float32) / 2147483648.0

    skip = int(latency_ms * RECORD_RATE / 1000)
    new = new[skip:]
    if len(new) < RECORD_RATE // 10:
        raise EditError("The take is too short after latency compensation.")

    if rate != RECORD_RATE:
        new = resample(new, RECORD_RATE / rate)
    new = match_channels(new, audio.shape[1]) * (10.0 ** (new_db / 20.0))
    base_gain = 10.0 ** (base_db / 20.0)

    total = max(len(audio), len(new))
    out = np.zeros((total, audio.shape[1]), dtype=np.float32)
    if loop_it:
        reps = math.ceil(total / len(audio))
        out += np.tile(audio, (reps, 1))[:total] * base_gain
    else:
        out[:len(audio)] += audio * base_gain
    out[:len(new)] += new

    peak = float(np.max(np.abs(out)))
    print(f"Peak of the mix: {to_db(peak):.1f} dB")
    if peak > 1.0:
        print("The mix is over full scale and will clip. Lower a level and run "
              "it again, or keep it and use normalize first on the layers.")
    return out


# ---------- stereo dynamics ----------

def do_channel_switch(audio, rate):
    if audio.shape[1] != 2:
        raise EditError("Channel switch needs a stereo file.")

    duration = len(audio) / rate
    print(f"""
CHANNEL SWITCH
Plays the sound from one channel only, then from the other channel only,
then returns to stereo. The pattern can repeat until the end.
When one channel plays alone it carries the mono mix of both sides.

File length: {format_time(duration)}

  Start with: which channel plays alone first (l or r).
  Start at: before this time the sound stays in stereo.
  First / Second channel only / Back to stereo: how long each part
    lasts, in seconds. Each default is the time you typed just before it.
  Cycles: 0 repeats the pattern until the end of the file. 1 plays it
    once and then stays in stereo.
  Transition: a short crossfade at each switch. 5-20 ms is a clean cut,
    200+ ms is a slow sweep between sides.
""")
    while True:
        try:
            answer = input("Start with which channel? (l/r) [l]: ").strip().lower()
        except EOFError:
            raise SystemExit(0)
        if answer in ("", "l", "left"):
            first_state, first_name, second_state, second_name = "L", "Left", "R", "Right"
            break
        if answer in ("r", "right"):
            first_state, first_name, second_state, second_name = "R", "Right", "L", "Left"
            break
        print("Enter l for left or r for right.")

    start = ask("Start at", 0.0, 0.0, max(0.0, duration - 0.001), is_time=True)
    first_s = ask(f"{first_name} channel only (seconds)", 5, 0.01, 3600)
    second_s = ask(f"{second_name} channel only (seconds)", first_s, 0.01, 3600)
    stereo_s = ask("Back to stereo (seconds)", second_s, 0.01, 3600)
    cycles = int(ask("Cycles (0 = repeat until the end)", 0, 0, 100000))
    fade_ms = ask("Transition (ms)", 20, 1, 2000)

    # Each state is (mono on left, mono on right, original stereo)
    weights = {"L": (1.0, 0.0, 0.0), "R": (0.0, 1.0, 0.0), "S": (0.0, 0.0, 1.0)}

    segments: list[tuple[float, str]] = []
    t = 0.0
    if start > 0:
        segments.append((0.0, "S"))
        t = start
    done = 0
    while t < duration and (cycles == 0 or done < cycles):
        for state, length in ((first_state, first_s),
                              (second_state, second_s),
                              ("S", stereo_s)):
            segments.append((t, state))
            t += length
        done += 1

    lengths = [first_s, second_s, stereo_s] + ([start] if start > 0 else [])
    fade = min(fade_ms / 1000.0, 0.5 * min(lengths))

    xs = [0.0]
    ys = [weights[segments[0][1]]]
    for i in range(1, len(segments)):
        when = segments[i][0]
        xs += [when - fade / 2, when + fade / 2]
        ys += [weights[segments[i - 1][1]], weights[segments[i][1]]]
    ys_arr = np.array(ys, dtype=np.float64)

    times = np.arange(len(audio), dtype=np.float64) / rate
    w = [np.interp(times, xs, ys_arr[:, k]).astype(np.float32) for k in range(3)]

    mono = audio.mean(axis=1)
    out = np.empty_like(audio)
    out[:, 0] = w[0] * mono + w[2] * audio[:, 0]
    out[:, 1] = w[1] * mono + w[2] * audio[:, 1]

    repeat = "until the end" if cycles == 0 else f"{cycles} cycle(s)"
    print(f"Pattern: {first_name.lower()} {first_s:g}s, {second_name.lower()} "
          f"{second_s:g}s, stereo {stereo_s:g}s, {repeat}.")
    return out


# ---------- mastering ----------

def do_peak_report(audio, rate):
    mag = np.abs(audio).max(axis=1)
    peak = float(mag.max())
    if peak <= 0:
        raise EditError("The file is silent.")
    total = len(mag)
    peak_db = to_db(peak)
    rms = float(np.sqrt(np.mean(audio.astype(np.float64) ** 2)))
    over0 = int(np.count_nonzero(mag > 1.0))

    print(f"""
PEAK REPORT
Length:        {format_time(total / rate)}
Sample peak:   {peak_db:+.2f} dB
Average (RMS): {to_db(rms):+.2f} dB
Crest factor:  {peak_db - to_db(rms):.1f} dB  (peak minus average; a high
               number means a few peaks set the level)
Over 0 dB:     {over0} samples ({100.0 * over0 / total:.3f}%)

If you cut the peaks at a level, then set the final peak to -1 dB:
  Cut at (dB)   Samples cut   Louder than plain normalize""")
    for drop in (0, 1, 3, 6, 9, 12):
        level = peak / (10.0 ** (drop / 20.0))
        cut = int(np.count_nonzero(mag > level))
        print(f"  {to_db(level):+8.2f}     {100.0 * cut / total:9.4f}%      "
              f"{'+' if drop else ''}{drop} dB")

    seconds = total // rate
    if seconds > 0:
        blocks = mag[:seconds * rate].reshape(seconds, rate).max(axis=1)
        near = int(np.count_nonzero(blocks >= peak * 0.708))
        print(f"\nSeconds with a peak within 3 dB of the maximum: {near} of {seconds}")
        print("(Few = isolated spikes set the level. Many = the sustained sound is that loud.)")
        top = sorted(int(i) for i in np.argsort(blocks)[::-1][:8])
        print("Loudest moments: " + ", ".join(
            f"{format_time(i)} ({to_db(float(blocks[i])):+.1f})" for i in top))

    raise EditError("Report only. No file was made.")


def do_ceiling_clip(audio, rate):
    peak = float(np.max(np.abs(audio)))
    if peak <= 0:
        raise EditError("The file is silent.")
    peak_db = to_db(peak)
    print(f"""
CEILING CLIP
Cuts only the samples above the clip level, flat and instantly (no
release, no ducking). Everything below it is untouched. Then the file is
scaled so the clip level becomes the final peak.

Current peak: {peak_db:+.2f} dB

  Clip level (dB): peaks above this are cut. 0 cuts only what is over full
    scale. Lower values cut more and make the result louder. Run
    peak-report first to see how many samples each level affects.
  Final peak (dB): where the loudest sample ends up. -1 leaves safe room.
    To only cut, without changing the level, set it equal to the clip
    level (for example both 0).
""")
    default_clip = 0.0 if peak_db > 0 else peak_db - 3.0
    clip_db = ask("Clip level (dB)", default_clip, -60, peak_db)
    level = 10.0 ** (clip_db / 20.0)
    target_db = ask("Final peak (dB)", -1.0, -30, 0)

    mag = np.abs(audio).max(axis=1)
    pct = 100.0 * np.count_nonzero(mag > level) / len(mag)
    out = np.clip(audio, -level, level) * (10.0 ** (target_db / 20.0) / level)

    print(f"Samples cut: {pct:.4f}%")
    print(f"Gain applied: {target_db - clip_db:+.2f} dB "
          f"(plain normalize would apply {target_db - peak_db:+.2f} dB)")
    if pct > 1.0:
        print("More than 1% of samples were cut. Listen for a clipped sound.")
    return out


# ---------- repair edits ----------

def spectral_denoise(x, noise_mag, floor, n_fft, hop, window):
    """Spectral-subtraction gate for one channel."""
    x = x.astype(np.float64)
    pad = n_fft
    xp = np.concatenate([np.zeros(pad), x, np.zeros(pad + n_fft)])
    view = np.lib.stride_tricks.sliding_window_view(xp, n_fft)[::hop]
    n_frames = len(view)
    out = np.zeros(len(xp))

    for b in range(0, n_frames, 1024):
        frames = view[b:b + 1024] * window
        spec = np.fft.rfft(frames, axis=1)
        mag2 = np.maximum(np.abs(spec) ** 2, 1e-12)
        gain = 1.0 - (1.5 * noise_mag) ** 2 / mag2
        gain = np.sqrt(np.clip(gain, floor ** 2, 1.0))
        gain = (np.roll(gain, 1, axis=1) + gain + np.roll(gain, -1, axis=1)) / 3.0
        new = np.fft.irfft(spec * gain, n_fft, axis=1) * window
        for j in range(len(new)):
            start = (b + j) * hop
            out[start:start + n_fft] += new[j]

    return (out / 1.5)[pad:pad + len(x)].astype(np.float32)


def do_denoise(audio, rate):
    n_fft, hop = 2048, 512
    window = 0.5 - 0.5 * np.cos(2.0 * np.pi * np.arange(n_fft) / n_fft)
    if len(audio) < n_fft * 4:
        raise EditError("The file is too short for noise reduction.")

    print("""
DENOISE
Learns what the hiss sounds like from a short stretch of "noise only"
audio, then turns that sound down everywhere else.

  Reduction (dB): how much the hiss is lowered.
    6-10  = slight. Keeps the sound natural.
    12-20 = strong. Can make quiet parts sound watery or hollow.

  Noise sample: a spot where only hiss is playing (no music), for
  example the silence before a tape starts. Type a start time to use
  half a second from there, or press Enter to use the quietest half
  second of the file.
""")
    reduction_db = ask("Reduction in dB", 8, 1, 30)

    block = min(len(audio), int(0.5 * rate))
    try:
        answer = input("Noise sample start (Enter = automatic): ").strip()
    except EOFError:
        raise SystemExit(0)

    if answer:
        try:
            start = int(parse_time(answer) * rate)
        except ValueError:
            raise EditError("Could not read that time.")
        start = max(0, min(start, len(audio) - block))
    else:
        level = np.concatenate([[0.0], np.cumsum(np.mean(np.abs(audio), axis=1))])
        start = int(np.argmin(level[block:] - level[:-block]))

    print(f"Using noise sample at {format_time(start / rate)}")

    floor = 10.0 ** (-reduction_db / 20.0)
    channels = []
    for c in range(audio.shape[1]):
        segment = audio[start:start + block, c].astype(np.float64)
        frames = np.lib.stride_tricks.sliding_window_view(segment, n_fft)[::hop]
        noise_mag = np.abs(np.fft.rfft(frames * window, axis=1)).mean(axis=0)
        print(f"Cleaning channel {c + 1} of {audio.shape[1]}...")
        channels.append(spectral_denoise(audio[:, c], noise_mag, floor,
                                         n_fft, hop, window))
    return np.stack(channels, axis=1)


def do_declick(audio, rate):
    print("""
DECLICK
Finds very short spikes and replaces them with a smooth line between the
samples on either side.

  Sensitivity: how big a spike must be, compared with the normal signal
  around it, to count as a click.
    5-8   = aggressive. Catches small crackle. May dull sharp attacks.
    10-15 = balanced.
    25+   = only obvious loud clicks.
  Harsh noise and hard transients can look like clicks, so start high
  and lower the number in steps.
""")
    sensitivity = ask("Sensitivity", 12, 3, 60)

    out = audio.copy()
    total = 0
    block = 2048
    max_run = max(3, int(0.002 * rate))      # repair only runs up to 2 ms

    for c in range(out.shape[1]):
        x = out[:, c]
        if len(x) < block * 2:
            continue

        d2 = np.abs(2 * x[1:-1] - x[:-2] - x[2:])
        d2 = np.concatenate([[0.0], d2, [0.0]])

        padded = np.concatenate([d2, np.zeros((-len(d2)) % block)])
        medians = np.median(padded.reshape(-1, block), axis=1)
        scale = np.repeat(medians, block)[:len(d2)]

        hit = (d2 > sensitivity * np.maximum(scale, 1e-5)) & (d2 > 0.004)
        hit = np.convolve(hit.astype(np.float32), np.ones(5), mode="same") > 0

        edges = np.diff(np.concatenate([[0], hit.astype(np.int8), [0]]))
        starts = np.flatnonzero(edges == 1)
        ends = np.flatnonzero(edges == -1)
        for s, e in zip(starts, ends):
            if e - s > max_run:
                hit[s:e] = False
            else:
                total += 1

        if hit.any():
            good = np.flatnonzero(~hit)
            bad = np.flatnonzero(hit)
            x[bad] = np.interp(bad, good, x[good])

    if total == 0:
        raise EditError("No clicks found. Try a lower sensitivity.")
    print(f"Repaired {total} clicks.")
    return out


def do_declip(audio, rate):
    out = audio.copy()
    total = 0

    for c in range(out.shape[1]):
        x = out[:, c]
        peak = float(np.max(np.abs(x)))
        if peak <= 0:
            continue
        hit = (np.abs(x) >= peak * 0.985).astype(np.int8)
        edges = np.diff(np.concatenate([[0], hit, [0]]))
        starts = np.flatnonzero(edges == 1)
        ends = np.flatnonzero(edges == -1)

        for s, e in zip(starts, ends):
            if e - s < 3 or e - s > 200 or s < 2 or e > len(x) - 2:
                continue
            y0, y1 = float(x[s - 1]), float(x[e])
            m0 = float(x[s - 1] - x[s - 2])
            m1 = float(x[e + 1] - x[e])
            span = e - s + 1
            t = np.arange(1, span) / span
            h00 = 2 * t**3 - 3 * t**2 + 1
            h10 = t**3 - 2 * t**2 + t
            h01 = -2 * t**3 + 3 * t**2
            h11 = t**3 - t**2
            x[s:e] = np.clip(h00 * y0 + h10 * span * m0 + h01 * y1 + h11 * span * m1,
                             -2 * peak, 2 * peak)
            total += 1

    if total == 0:
        raise EditError("No clipped peaks found.")

    new_peak = float(np.max(np.abs(out)))
    print(f"Rebuilt {total} clipped peaks.")
    if new_peak > 0.89:
        out *= 0.89 / new_peak
        print(f"Level lowered by {20 * math.log10(new_peak / 0.89):.1f} dB "
              "to make room for the restored peaks.")
    return out


EDITS = {
    "extract": do_extract,
    "trim-silence": do_trim_silence,
    "reverse": do_reverse,
    "fade": do_fade,
    "normalize": do_normalize,
    "gain-up": do_gain_up,
    "gain-down": do_gain_down,
    "gain-crush": do_gain_crush,
    "denoise": do_denoise,
    "declick": do_declick,
    "declip": do_declip,
    "remove-dc": do_remove_dc,
    "varispeed": do_varispeed,
    "stretch": do_stretch,
    "stereo-width": do_stereo_width,
    "loop": do_loop,
    "loop-section": do_loop_section,
    "layer": do_layer,
    "overdub": do_overdub,
    "channel-switch": do_channel_switch,
    "peak-report": do_peak_report,
    "ceiling-clip": do_ceiling_clip,
}


# ---------- file picker ----------

def show_info(path_text: str) -> None:
    """Print file details. Called by the fzf preview pane."""
    path = Path(path_text)
    try:
        info = sf.info(path)
    except Exception as error:
        print(f"Cannot read file: {error}")
        return
    size_mb = path.stat().st_size / (1024 * 1024)
    print(path.name)
    print()
    print(f"Duration:     {format_time(info.frames / info.samplerate)}")
    print(f"Sample rate:  {info.samplerate} Hz")
    print(f"Channels:     {info.channels}")
    print(f"Format:       {info.format} / {info.subtype}")
    print(f"Size:         {size_mb:.2f} MB")
    print(f"Folder:       {path.parent.resolve()}")


def find_audio_files(folder: Path) -> list[str]:
    """WAV/FLAC files directly inside folder (no subfolders), newest first."""
    found: list[tuple[float, str]] = []
    try:
        entries = list(os.scandir(folder))
    except OSError as error:
        fail(f"Cannot read folder {folder}: {error}")
    for entry in entries:
        if entry.name.startswith(".") or not entry.is_file():
            continue
        if Path(entry.name).suffix.lower() in SUPPORTED:
            found.append((entry.stat().st_mtime, entry.name))
    found.sort(reverse=True)
    return [name for _, name in found]


def choose_file(folder: Path, prompt: str = "File> ") -> Path | None:
    files = find_audio_files(folder)
    if not files:
        raise EditError(f"No WAV or FLAC files in: {folder}")

    os.chdir(folder)
    script = shlex.quote(str(Path(__file__).resolve()))
    python = shlex.quote(sys.executable)

    try:
        result = subprocess.run(
            ["fzf", f"--prompt={prompt}", "--height=60%", "--layout=reverse",
             "--border", "--no-multi",
             f"--header={folder}   |   hover plays   Enter: select   "
             "Ctrl-R: replay   Esc: cancel",
             f"--preview={python} {script} --info {{}}",
             "--preview-window=right:50%:wrap",
             f"--bind=focus:execute-silent({python} {script} --preview-play {{}})",
             f"--bind=ctrl-r:execute-silent({python} {script} --preview-play {{}})"],
            input="\n".join(files), text=True, stdout=subprocess.PIPE,
        )
    except FileNotFoundError:
        fail("fzf was not found in PATH.")
    finally:
        stop_preview()

    choice = result.stdout.strip()
    return (folder / choice).resolve() if choice else None


# ---------- UI ----------

def pick_process(current: Path, steps: int) -> str | None:
    entries = {**PROCESSES, **SPECIAL}
    lines = "\n".join(f"{name}\t{desc}" for name, desc in entries.items())
    header = (f"Working on: {current.name}   |   kept edits this session: {steps}"
              "   |   Esc = finish")
    try:
        result = subprocess.run(
            ["fzf", "--prompt=Edit> ", "--height=60%", "--layout=reverse",
             "--border", "--no-multi", "--delimiter=\t", "--with-nth=1",
             f"--header={header}",
             "--preview=echo {2}", "--preview-window=down:3:wrap"],
            input=lines, text=True, stdout=subprocess.PIPE,
        )
    except FileNotFoundError:
        fail("fzf was not found in PATH.")
    choice = result.stdout.strip().split("\t")[0]
    return choice or None


def play(path: Path) -> None:
    command = ["mpv", "--force-window=yes", f"--title={path.name}"]
    if VISUAL:
        command.append(f"--lavfi-complex={VISUAL_FILTER}")
    else:
        command.append("--no-video")
    command += ["--", str(path)]
    try:
        subprocess.run(command)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        fail("mpv was not found in PATH.")


def review(previous: Path) -> str:
    actions = {"k": "keep", "f": "finish", "d": "discard", "r": "again",
               "a": "another", "l": "listen", "q": "quit"}
    while True:
        try:
            c = input(
                "\n  [k] keep and continue editing"
                "\n  [f] finish with this version"
                f"\n  [d] discard it, go back to: {previous.name}"
                "\n  [r] run again   [a] another edit   [l] listen"
                "\n  [q] quit without this render"
                "\n> ").strip().lower()
        except EOFError:
            return "quit"
        if c in actions:
            return actions[c]
        print("Enter k, f, d, r, a, l, or q.")


def record_and_confirm() -> Path | None:
    """Record, play back, and ask whether to keep the take."""
    while True:
        recorded = record_take()
        if recorded is None:
            return None
        current = recorded.resolve()
        play(current)

        decision = ""
        while decision not in ("k", "d", "r"):
            try:
                decision = input("\nRecording: [k]eep and edit / [d]iscard / "
                                 "[r]e-record / [l]isten: ").strip().lower()
            except EOFError:
                decision = "k"
            if decision == "l":
                play(current)

        if decision == "k":
            return current
        current.unlink(missing_ok=True)
        print("Discarded.")
        if decision == "d":
            return None


def organize_recordings() -> None:
    """Move recording_YYYYMMDD_* files into YYYYMMDD folders."""
    if not RECORD_DIR.is_dir():
        fail(f"Folder not found: {RECORD_DIR}")
    pattern = re.compile(r"^recording_(\d{8})_")
    moved = skipped = 0
    for item in sorted(RECORD_DIR.iterdir()):
        if not item.is_file():
            continue
        match = pattern.match(item.name)
        if not match:
            continue
        day_dir = RECORD_DIR / match.group(1)
        dest = day_dir / item.name
        if dest.exists():
            print(f"Skipped (already exists): {item.name}")
            skipped += 1
            continue
        day_dir.mkdir(exist_ok=True)
        shutil.move(str(item), str(dest))
        print(f"{item.name}  ->  {match.group(1)}/")
        moved += 1
    print(f"\nMoved {moved} file(s), skipped {skipped}.")


def album_master(folder: Path, target_db: float) -> None:
    """Apply ONE shared gain to every track so relative levels are kept."""
    if not folder.is_dir():
        fail(f"Not a folder: {folder}")
    names = sorted(find_audio_files(folder))
    if not names:
        fail(f"No WAV or FLAC files in: {folder}")

    print(f"\nALBUM MASTER: {folder}")
    print("Reading tracks...\n")
    info: list[tuple[str, float, float]] = []
    for name in names:
        audio, _ = load_audio(folder / name)
        peak = float(np.max(np.abs(audio)))
        rms = float(np.sqrt(np.mean(audio.astype(np.float64) ** 2)))
        info.append((name, to_db(peak), to_db(rms)))

    loudest = max(p for _, p, _ in info)
    gain_db = target_db - loudest
    gain = 10.0 ** (gain_db / 20.0)

    print(f"{'Track':<40} {'Peak':>8} {'Average':>9} {'New peak':>9}")
    for name, p, r in info:
        print(f"{name[:40]:<40} {p:+8.2f} {r:+9.2f} {p + gain_db:+9.2f}")
    print(f"\nLoudest peak in the album: {loudest:+.2f} dB")
    print(f"Shared gain for every track: {gain_db:+.2f} dB")
    print(f"The loudest track will peak at {target_db:+.2f} dB. Relative levels "
          "between tracks are unchanged.")
    if gain_db > 0:
        print("Note: the gain is positive, so the album gets louder.")

    out_dir = folder / "mastered"
    print(f"\nMastered copies go to: {out_dir}")
    print("Originals are not changed.")
    try:
        answer = input("Write the mastered files? [y/N]: ").strip().lower()
    except EOFError:
        answer = ""
    if answer != "y":
        print("Nothing written.")
        return

    out_dir.mkdir(exist_ok=True)
    for name, _, _ in info:
        dest = out_dir / name
        if dest.exists():
            print(f"Skipped (already exists): {name}")
            continue
        audio, rate = load_audio(folder / name)
        save_audio(dest, (audio * gain).astype(np.float32), rate)
        print(f"Written: {dest.name}")
    print("\nDone.")


def parse_args() -> argparse.Namespace:
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog="audio_edit.py",
        description="Record and quickly edit audio (WAV and FLAC).",
        epilog=HELP_TEXT,
        formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter,
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "target", nargs="?",
        help="A WAV/FLAC file, or a folder to pick from. "
             "With no argument the script records a new take.",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--keep-all", action="store_true",
        help="Keep every render of the session instead of only the last one.",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--plain", action="store_true",
        help="Use a plain mpv window without the level meter and spectrum.",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--meter", action="store_true",
        help="Show the live input level meter first (nothing is recorded), "
             "then continue to the record prompt.",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--organize", action="store_true",
        help="Move existing recordings into YYYYMMDD folders, then exit.",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--album", metavar="FOLDER",
        help="Apply one shared gain to every track in FOLDER and write "
             "24-bit copies into FOLDER/mastered/, then exit.",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--album-peak", type=float, default=-1.0, metavar="DB",
        help="Final peak of the loudest album track in dB (default -1).",
    )
    parser.add_argument("--info", metavar="FILE", help=argparse.SUPPRESS)
    parser.add_argument("--preview-play", metavar="FILE", help=argparse.SUPPRESS)
    return parser.parse_args()


def main() -> None:
    global VISUAL
    args = parse_args()

    if args.info:
        show_info(args.info)
        return
    if args.preview_play:
        start_preview(args.preview_play)
        return
    if args.organize:
        organize_recordings()
        return
    if args.album:
        if args.album_peak > 0:
            fail("--album-peak must be 0 or lower.")
        album_master(Path(args.album).expanduser().resolve(), args.album_peak)
        return

    if args.plain:
        VISUAL = False

    if args.meter:
        meter_mode()
        if args.target is None:
            print("\nContinuing to recording.")

    if args.target is None:
        start = record_and_confirm()
        if start is None:
            return
    else:
        target = Path(args.target).expanduser().resolve()
        if target.is_dir():
            try:
                picked = choose_file(target)
            except EditError as error:
                fail(str(error))
            if picked is None:
                return
            start = picked
        elif target.is_file():
            if target.suffix.lower() not in SUPPORTED:
                fail("Only WAV and FLAC files are supported.")
            start = target
        else:
            fail(f"Not found: {target}")

    history: list[Path] = [start]     # kept versions, oldest first
    made: set[Path] = set()           # renders created by this session
    process: str | None = None

    def remove_render(path: Path) -> None:
        if path in made:
            path.unlink(missing_ok=True)
            made.discard(path)

    while True:
        current = history[-1]
        audio, rate = load_audio(current)
        CONTEXT["folder"] = current.parent
        print(f"\nWorking version: {current.name}  "
              f"({format_time(len(audio) / rate)})")

        if process is None:
            choice = pick_process(current, len(history) - 1)
            if choice is None or choice == "finish":
                break
            if choice == "undo":
                if len(history) <= 1:
                    print("Nothing to undo. You are on the starting file.")
                else:
                    removed = history.pop()
                    remove_render(removed)
                    print(f"Undone: removed {removed.name}")
                    print(f"Back to: {history[-1].name}")
                continue
            process = choice

        output = next_output(current, process)

        try:
            rendered = EDITS[process](audio, rate)
        except EditError as error:
            print(f"\n{error}")
            process = None
            continue

        rendered = np.clip(rendered, -1.0, 1.0).astype(np.float32)
        save_audio(output, rendered, rate)
        play(output)

        finished = False

        while True:
            action = review(current)

            if action == "listen":
                play(output)
                continue

            if action == "keep":
                history.append(output)
                made.add(output)
                process = None
                print(f"Kept. Now working on: {output.name}")
            elif action == "finish":
                history.append(output)
                made.add(output)
                finished = True
            elif action == "discard":
                output.unlink(missing_ok=True)
                process = None
                print(f"Discarded. Still on: {current.name}")
            elif action == "again":
                output.unlink(missing_ok=True)
                print(f"Running {process} again.")
            elif action == "another":
                output.unlink(missing_ok=True)
                process = None
            else:
                output.unlink(missing_ok=True)
                print(f"Render discarded. Finishing with: {current.name}")
                finished = True
            break

        if finished:
            break

    final = history[-1]
    if not args.keep_all:
        for old in history[:-1]:
            if old in made:
                old.unlink(missing_ok=True)
                print(f"Removed intermediate: {old.name}")

    print(f"\nFinal file: {final}")


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
